---

# Clarify
- O(1) get and put.
- Capacity fixed at construction; evict least-recently-used on overflow.
- `get` on a key — does it refresh recency? **Yes**.
- Negative capacity? Ignore — LC guarantees ≥ 1.

# Approach
Hash map + doubly linked list.
- Hash: `key → node`
- DLL: head = most-recent, tail = least-recent (or vice versa; pick one and stay consistent)
- `get`: look up, move node to head, return value.
- `put`: insert at head; if over capacity, remove tail node from DLL and map.

Why not `LinkedHashMap`? In Java you *can* — override `removeEldestEntry`. But in an interview, implement the DLL yourself to show you understand the invariant.

# Java (hand-rolled)
```java
class LRUCache {
    private static class Node {
        int k, v; Node prev, next;
        Node(int k, int v) { this.k = k; this.v = v; }
    }
    private final int cap;
    private final Map<Integer, Node> map = new HashMap<>();
    private final Node head = new Node(0,0), tail = new Node(0,0);

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.cap = capacity;
        head.next = tail; tail.prev = head;
    }
    public int get(int key) {
        Node n = map.get(key);
        if (n == null) return -1;
        remove(n); addFirst(n);
        return n.v;
    }
    public void put(int key, int value) {
        Node n = map.get(key);
        if (n != null) { n.v = value; remove(n); addFirst(n); return; }
        if (map.size() == cap) {
            Node lru = tail.prev;
            remove(lru); map.remove(lru.k);
        }
        Node fresh = new Node(key, value);
        addFirst(fresh); map.put(key, fresh);
    }
    private void addFirst(Node n) {
        n.next = head.next; n.prev = head;
        head.next.prev = n; head.next = n;
    }
    private void remove(Node n) {
        n.prev.next = n.next; n.next.prev = n.prev;
    }
}
```

# Edge cases
- Repeated `put` with same key → update value, bump recency, do NOT grow size.
- Capacity 1 → every put evicts the previous.
- `get` on absent key → `-1`.

# Complexity
- `get` / `put` → **O(1) time** (amortised O(1) for the hashmap).
- Space: **O(capacity)**.

# Follow-ups (expect every one)
1. **Add TTL.** Each node carries `expiresAt`. On `get`, if expired → treat as miss, lazy-remove. A background sweeper purges when idle. (Speechify asks this.)
2. **Thread-safe.** Guard with a single `ReentrantLock`, or shard the cache into N stripes keyed by `hash(key) % N` and lock per stripe (lower contention; what real KV caches do).
3. **Sharded KV-cache across inference workers.** Consistent hashing on `prompt_prefix_hash → worker`; each worker owns its LRU; evictions never cross machines. Mention cache-aware routing (vLLM's prefix caching).
4. **LFU instead.** → LC 460; different data structure (counters + per-count DLL).
5. **Observability.** Expose `hit_rate`, `evictions_per_sec`, `avg_entry_age`. In the AI-serving context, hit rate on the *first N tokens* of the prompt is the metric that matters.

# Walk-through
```
cap=2
put(1,1)  → {1:1}
put(2,2)  → {1:1, 2:2}
get(1)    → 1, bump 1 to head → order: [1, 2]
put(3,3)  → evict 2 → {1:1, 3:3}
get(2)    → -1
put(4,4)  → evict 1 → {3:3, 4:4}
get(1)    → -1
get(3)    → 3
get(4)    → 4
```

# AI-native overlay
Modern LLM serving (vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, SGLang) does exactly this at the GPU memory layer:
- Key = prompt-prefix hash
- Value = KV tensors for those tokens
- Eviction = LRU (sometimes LFU for stable prompts)
- Sharded across workers for horizontal scale

When asked, don't just describe the DS — explain **why** LLM serving needs it (reusing KV avoids re-encoding; multi-tenant prompts share prefixes like system messages). That's the Anthropic/OpenAI signal.

// LRU Cache — the AI-infra interview question.
// Target: Anthropic, OpenAI, Scale, Speechify, Together AI.
// O(1) get and put via HashMap + doubly linked list.
// For TTL variant, add `long expiresAt` to Node; lazy-remove on get.

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class LRUCache {
    private static class Node {
        int k, v;
        Node prev, next;
        Node(int k, int v) { this.k = k; this.v = v; }
    }

    private final int cap;
    private final Map<Integer, Node> map = new HashMap<>();
    private final Node head = new Node(0, 0);
    private final Node tail = new Node(0, 0);

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.cap = capacity;
        head.next = tail;
        tail.prev = head;
    }

    public int get(int key) {
        Node n = map.get(key);
        if (n == null) return -1;
        remove(n);
        addFirst(n);
        return n.v;
    }

    public void put(int key, int value) {
        Node n = map.get(key);
        if (n != null) {
            n.v = value;
            remove(n);
            addFirst(n);
            return;
        }
        if (map.size() == cap) {
            Node lru = tail.prev;
            remove(lru);
            map.remove(lru.k);
        }
        Node fresh = new Node(key, value);
        addFirst(fresh);
        map.put(key, fresh);
    }

    private void addFirst(Node n) {
        n.next = head.next;
        n.prev = head;
        head.next.prev = n;
        head.next = n;
    }

    private void remove(Node n) {
        n.prev.next = n.next;
        n.next.prev = n.prev;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LRUCache c = new LRUCache(2);
        c.put(1, 1);
        c.put(2, 2);
        System.out.println(c.get(1));   // 1
        c.put(3, 3);                     // evicts 2
        System.out.println(c.get(2));   // -1
        c.put(4, 4);                     // evicts 1
        System.out.println(c.get(1));   // -1
        System.out.println(c.get(3));   // 3
        System.out.println(c.get(4));   // 4
    }
}
